course: dictionary
lesson: 7344
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives «plate» several meanings, so the words around it tell you which one you need. Always read the whole sentence before you choose.
explain: «Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.» Here a number plate is a flat piece of metal with letters and numbers on a car. There is no food in this sentence, so the dish meaning does not fit.
explain: «Clean your plate» is said at dinner, so here plate means the flat dish you put food on. To check your choice, swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Plate is a noun (plate, plates). It can also be a verb, meaning to cover something with a thin layer of metal.
fact: A plate can be a flat dish you put food on, or a flat thin piece of metal, glass or other hard material.
fact: In science, the Earth's surface is made of huge plates that move slowly. These are called tectonic plates.
fact: In baseball, home plate is where the batter stands.
fact: In maths, plates can be a container: if 12 biscuits go on plates with 4 on each, you need 12 ÷ 4 = 3 plates.
exercise: In «Clean your plate», what does plate mean? | *A flat dish you put food on | Home base in baseball | A huge slow-moving part of the Earth | A flat thin piece of metal
exercise: «Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.» What is a number plate? | A bowl of soup | A tall glass for water | *A flat metal sign with a car's letters and numbers | A flat dish for food
exercise: We put 12 biscuits on plates, with 4 biscuits on each plate. How many plates do we need? | *3 | 48 | 8 | 2
test: Which meaning fits «the Earth's surface is made of huge plates that move slowly»? | A thin layer of metal on a bike | A flat dish for food | *A huge, slow-moving part of the Earth's surface | A number plate on a car
test: Plate can be a verb. What does it mean to plate something? | *To cover it with a thin layer of metal | To throw it away | To wash it in hot water | To eat it quickly
exam: In «too much on my plate», what does plate mean? | Too many sheets of metal | Too many fish in the sea | *Too much to do | Too much food on my dish
